### 7.6 Chain of Responsibility Pattern

**Intent:** Pass a request along a chain of handlers; each handler decides to process or pass it on.

```java
public abstract class AuthHandler {
    protected AuthHandler next;
    public AuthHandler setNext(AuthHandler next) { this.next = next; return next; }
    public abstract void handle(Request request);
}

public class AuthenticationHandler extends AuthHandler {
    public void handle(Request request) {
        if (!request.isAuthenticated()) {
            System.out.println("Authentication failed!");
        } else if (next != null) {
            next.handle(request);
        }
    }
}

public class AuthorizationHandler extends AuthHandler {
    public void handle(Request request) {
        if (!request.hasPermission()) {
            System.out.println("Authorization failed!");
        } else if (next != null) {
            next.handle(request);
        }
    }
}

public class RateLimitHandler extends AuthHandler {
    public void handle(Request request) {
        if (request.isRateLimited()) {
            System.out.println("Rate limit exceeded!");
        } else if (next != null) {
            next.handle(request);
        }
    }
}

// Chain: Auth -> Authz -> RateLimit
AuthHandler auth = new AuthenticationHandler();
auth.setNext(new AuthorizationHandler()).setNext(new RateLimitHandler());
auth.handle(request);
```

