## 9. Class Design & Relationships

### Composition vs Aggregation vs Association

| | Association | Aggregation | Composition |
|-|-------------|-------------|-------------|
| **Relationship** | Uses | Has-a (weak) | Has-a (strong) |
| **Lifecycle** | Independent | Independent | Dependent |
| **Example** | Student borrows Book | Department has Employee | House has Room |
| **If parent deleted** | Child survives | Child survives | Child is deleted |

```java
// Association
class Student {
    void borrow(Library library) { ... }  // uses library temporarily
}

// Aggregation
class Department {
    List<Employee> employees;  // employees can exist without department
}

// Composition
class House {
    private final List<Room> rooms;
    public House() { rooms = new ArrayList<>(); rooms.add(new Room()); }
    // rooms cannot exist without House
}
```

### Inheritance vs Composition

> **"Favor composition over inheritance"**

| | Inheritance | Composition |
|-|-------------|-------------|
| **Coupling** | Tight | Loose |
| **Flexibility** | Low | High |
| **Reusability** | Limited | High |
| **Change impact** | High | Low |

```java
// Bad: Inheritance
class Stack extends ArrayList<Integer> {
    // Stack inherits all ArrayList methods (add, remove, etc.) — breaks abstraction
}

// Good: Composition
class Stack {
    private List<Integer> storage = new ArrayList<>();
    public void push(int x)   { storage.add(x); }
    public int  pop()         { return storage.remove(storage.size() - 1); }
    public int  peek()        { return storage.get(storage.size() - 1); }
    public boolean isEmpty()  { return storage.isEmpty(); }
}
```

