## 11. Clean Code Principles

### Naming Conventions

| Rule | Bad | Good |
|------|-----|------|
| Meaningful names | `int d;` | `int elapsedDays;` |
| Pronounceable | `genymdhms` | `generationTimestamp` |
| Avoid encodings | `strName` | `name` |
| Class = Noun | `DoPayment` | `PaymentProcessor` |
| Method = Verb | `data()` | `getData()` |
| Boolean = question | `flag` | `isActive`, `hasPermission` |

### Function Design

```java
// Bad: Does too much, no clear abstraction
public void processUser(User u) {
    // validate
    if (u.getName() == null || u.getEmail() == null) throw new Exception(...);
    // save to DB
    db.save(u);
    // send email
    emailService.send(u.getEmail(), "Welcome!");
    // log
    logger.info("User created: " + u.getId());
}

// Good: Single Responsibility, clear abstraction levels
public void registerUser(User user) {
    validateUser(user);
    saveUser(user);
    onboardUser(user);
}
```

### DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself

Extract duplicate code into a single abstraction.

### YAGNI — You Aren't Gonna Need It

Don't implement features until they are actually needed.

### KISS — Keep It Simple, Stupid

Prefer the simplest solution that works.

### Law of Demeter (Principle of Least Knowledge)

> A method should only call methods on:
> - itself
> - its parameters
> - objects it created
> - its direct fields

```java
// Bad: train wreck, violates LoD
user.getAddress().getCity().getName();

// Good: hide navigation
user.getCityName();
```

